            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> Other than writing own textobject (almost) from scratch, I don't know about the way to achieve that relatively pain free.\n\nWith only moderate amounts of pain, I was able to achieve a working implementation of this using `mini.ai`! It only triggers the new logic when necessary - in all other cases, it falls back to the `mini.comment` implementation.\n```lua\nrequire(\"mini.ai\").setup({\n  custom_textobjects = {\n    c = function()\n      local line = vim.api.nvim_get_current_line()\n\n      -- Get the commentstring up until %s (including the space).\n      local commentstr = vim.bo.commentstring:match(\"^(.*)%%s\")\n      -- Escape for use in regex. Did you know `-` has a meaning there? How\n      -- awful!\n      local escaped_commentstr = vim.pesc(commentstr)\n\n      -- If the line has a comment that's only following whitespace, we can\n      -- defer to mini.comment. We only have custom logic for EOL comments, since\n      -- mini.comment doesn't implement logic for them\n      if line:match(\"^%s*\" .. escaped_commentstr) then\n        require(\"mini.comment\").textobject()\n        return\n      end\n\n      local line_num = vim.fn.line(\".\")\n\n      local from = {\n        line = line_num,\n\n        -- The index where the commentstring is found\n        col = vim.fn.match(line, commentstr),\n      }\n\n      local to = {\n        line = line_num,\n\n      -- Actually not inclusive problems - for some reason, this returns 1 more\n      -- than the last index. Maybe for calculating string length? Anyways,\n      -- subtract 1.\n        col = vim.fn.col(\"$\") - 1,\n      }\n\n      return { from = from, to = to }\n    end,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nIt's not perfect - ideally, it would use a composed pattern and return the regex to match on directly. But it does seem to work in all my testing! If anyone has any improvements to it, feel free to share them.\n\n(Oh, and if you want separate `dic` / `cic` behavior, that lets you change a comment without deleting the first commentstring, I'll share an implementation I wrote for that in #1837. Yes, it still handles EOL comments)",
