            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> With only moderate amounts of pain, I was able to achieve a working implementation of this using `mini.ai`! It only triggers the new logic when necessary - in all other cases, it falls back to the `mini.comment` implementation.\n\nNice! There are couple of serious issues, though:\n- There is no check that there is actually a match. If there isn't, then it will a region with `from.col = -1`, which is according to specification. There needs to be an actual check that there is a match and return `nil` otherwise.\n- Using `vim.fn.match(line, commentstr)` still doesn't guarantee there is no issues with special characters (and `-` in Lua patterns is really useful, by the way). Like if `commentstr` contains `.`, for example.\n- `vim.fn.match()` returns zero-based indexing, while 'mini.ai' expects one-based. Not sure why it would be working here.\n\nThe better approach would be something like `local match_col = line:find(escaped_commentstr)` and checking that it is not `nil`.\n\nCouple of other observations:\n- Of course, this doesn't work if a match occurs somewhere where comment is not parsed as comment (as [mentioned earlier](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/1431#issuecomment-2562474661)). Like in the string. But that is the limitation of pure pattern based approach.\n- Instead of `vim.fn.col('$') - 1` it is possible to use `line:len()` (since there is already a current line stored in variable). And yes, `col('$')` does return \"length of cursor line plus one\", which is documented in `:h col()`. Mostly (I'd assume) because there is also a special \"end-of-line\" byte at the end and cursor can be positioned there (like in Insert and Visual modes).",
